WEBVTT

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:08.240
The story until now.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.810
The manga author Nozomu was arrested for violating established child pornography laws

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:14.540
entirely because the “Y” in “Young” stuck out on the top of his shirt.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.080
He's ignored by the publisher he thought would defend him, and his public defender dejectedly said,

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.140
“Guys like him rarely win against these kind of charges.”

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:24.030
Just then, a former cell phone developer in the witness stand stood and

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:29.300
held up his cell phone's antenna mark and shouted “Objection!”

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:35.080
“I came up with this mark first, so you should
charge him with trademark infringement too!”

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.300
“Eh?”

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.550
Nozomu is unanimously found guilty by
the newly implemented lay judge system,

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.240
and is sentenced to be imprisoned in the far north.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.660
Upon arrival, he was immediately
summoned to meet the head prisoner,

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.910
and from behind him a singing man resembling Mackey drew close.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:55.920
“I hate manga authors, but I think I'll enjoy you.”

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.290
If only this were a dream.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.030
Unfortunately, it wasn't.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:30.010
Credit titling

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:30.180
Censored and cut words

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:30.350
Clothing

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:30.510
Gag selection

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:30.680
Seasonality

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:30.950
At TaniOn, regretfully saying that Nami's keyframes are

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:37.390
Perfect.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.890
It's a beautiful work of art.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:44.760
I hate to tell you this,

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.770
but it's upside-down.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.290
Chiri's having a breakdown!

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.340
I'm really sorry, I didn't expect that.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:00.210
It happens.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:04.850
In fact, something similar happened in France.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.440
Monet's <i>Water Lilies</i> was displayed upside-down at an art museum,

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.860
and the art critics and museum staff failed to notice.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:15.020
Mistakes like these occur everywhere.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:17.860
People tend to notice trivial errors,

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.880
but fail to see the main problem.

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:24.970
It doesn't matter if she displays that piece upside-down or not.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:25.870
Kudo?

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:30.020
That's the point of this exhibition.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.880
It's an exhibition full of big mistakes that people didn't notice!

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:41.610
What's this?

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:43.020
A baseball.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:44.820
Str—

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:46.340
No, ball!

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.640
But you just said “strike”!

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:51.090
No, half the ball was outside.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.100
Damn those sharp eyes.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:54.040
And?

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.500
The umpire noticed that the ball was outside of the strike zone,

00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:00.120
but everyone failed to notice the number of outs.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.320
At four outs, they should've changed sides already.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:04.420
Change sides already!

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.150
True, that happens a lot.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:08.550
But why?

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:12.020
How can the entire stadium let the game proceed without noticing?

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:14.600
Hey, this looks familiar.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:22.860
It's my answer sheet from that test we just took.

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:25.880
It's an example of you missing big mistakes.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:29.880
Oh wait, this is a trick question.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:32.090
But I did notice it.

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:33.570
It's an incredibly normal mistake,

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.690
so I really hate to tell you this,

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:39.510
but you shifted the answer columns around.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:44.550
Here's a bigger surprise.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:46.550
Even if they weren't shifted around,

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:48.520
your score would be exactly the same.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:51.660
Enough.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.630
Those are my bandages...

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:58.670
I wonder why...

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:02.650
Owwie, it hurts.

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:05.290
I think I broke my pinky!

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.990
Your leg is pointing off somewhere funny!

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:11.740
Injured patients often end up with an adrenaline rush, making them unaware of the extent of their injuries.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:12.480
Brother!

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:15.060
They don't notice how serious their injuries really are!

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:17.060
I have a scrape on my arm.

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.070
I can't stop this nosebleed.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:20.950
My tummy hurts.

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:25.790
You call yourself a doctor!?

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:29.290
Oh, manga manuscripts.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:33.600
We're gonna take a jab at these?

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:34.910
You were there?

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:36.400
Yes, always.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:40.770
The manga industry is infamous
for failing to notice big mistakes!

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:44.010
Even though they manage to find and correct minor typos,

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:46.510
they still manage to completely miss the significant mistakes!

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.420
Let's reenact a real significant error.

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:50.730
Hito, step forward please.

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:51.520
Oh, me?

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:55.300
Lately, you've been going to the ramen joint by the station a lot, right?

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:57.220
Off course!

00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:01.350
I wanted to say “of course”, but I said “off course”!

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.420
I said “off course”!

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:06.600
A certain manga does that when it plays dirty tricks.{It's a trick phrase used in certain manga for enlightening purposes.}{don't really have any idea what either of these mean}

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:10.030
I realize I can't avoid tricks,

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:12.600
but I'd die if he made me say that!

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:16.170
There's plenty of famous lines caused by that kind of printing error!

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:20.850
Hey Nami, have you seen the new Evangelion movie yet?

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.550
Off course!

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:28.500
I saw a mistake like that on TV too.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:31.490
A really bad one.

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:35.560
The caption said “defective” instead of “defector!”

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:38.080
Huh, this is a panel with Otonashi.

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:39.950
Why Meru?

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:47.000
Usually, readers will notice a missing mole or toning,

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:51.580
but they'll miss bigger mistakes like someone acting completely out of character!

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:56.950
Teacher, Meru started writing a letter just now.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:01.370
That's great to hear.

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:02.690
I'm impressed!

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:09.560
Teacher, Meru started writing a letter just now.

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:11.890
That's great to hear.

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:13.200
I'm impressed!

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:18.200
Do you really think they didn't notice it?

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:23.310
I think they were just being kind and pretending they didn't.

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:26.240
Being kind and pretending they didn't?

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:33.750
That's right! This world is overflowing with the kindness of people who ignore big mistakes!

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:37.310
That's just wrong! I feel sorry for her!

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:41.260
When they discuss the little problems that occur in romantic relationships,

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:45.900
they are kind enough to pretend not to notice that they're actually talking about a 2D character.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.180
Hello, Mother?

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:48.980
I saw Nishi with Mobleeb.

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:50.730
It's Nioka.

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:55.550
You'll hear her say incorrect facts during the conversation,

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:53.750
Hello?

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:59.380
but you're kind enough to pretend not to notice that she isn't holding a phone.

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:57.320
Hello?

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:03.210
As I said before, people show their kindness by pretending not to notice the big mistakes.

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:04.720
As I said before, people show their kindness by pretending not to notice the big mistakes.

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:08.620
Now that I think of it, there's something that should be here, but isn't.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:10.170
Y'mean Meru's dad?

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:13.290
By now, he should have shown up complaining about his Meru-Meru getting erased...

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:15.630
Otonashi, what's the matter?

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:21.570
You erased Meru-Meru!

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:27.070
You didn't notice me because I was too big, right!?

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:31.580
If you lay all twelve pages end to end, Meru's dad will appear!

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:33.590
Gyaaaaaaa!

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.740
What do you think this is, a '60s monster flick!?

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:40.080
Y'mean the one where you think it's an island, but it turns out to be an animal?

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:41.120
Wanna run?

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:41.950
Off course!

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:48.760
The weather's perfect today!

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:51.700
Need to air things out every once in a while.

00:10:56.870 --> 00:10:59.340
My blanky! My precious blanky!

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:01.170
The appeal{interestingness} of the comic

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:01.340
The hard-working staff

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:01.510
The nudity of the creator

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:01.670
The nudity of the assistant
(his spirit of {inheritance?  pick a word.})

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:01.840
Ghostory's schedule

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:02.110
I still want a wife

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:10.450
I bought you a new blanket.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:16.990
It's much nicer than your old blanket.

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:19.810
I'm sure you'll like it.

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:21.930
I loved that one...!

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:25.980
I only need my blanky!

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:30.900
But sis, you've used different blankets before.

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:34.320
I changed the covers, but it was still the same blanky inside!

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:38.110
I don't feel secure without my blanky!

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:41.310
So it's a security blanket.

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:42.480
A security blanket?

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:48.960
Children often have comfort objects such as a toy or
spoon that they won't let go of even if it gets beat up.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.740
Just holding onto that item puts them at ease and makes them feel secure.

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:53.140
My favorite fork.

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:54.860
My favorite cotton blanket!

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:56.360
My favorite handkerchief!

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:58.720
I had a cotton blanket once too!

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:01.100
I would never let go of it.

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:03.370
It's so nostalgic.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:06.400
In Komori's case, it would be her blanket.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:08.510
Sniffle.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:10.510
So,

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:14.440
I'd like everyone to help me search for Komori's blanket.

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:17.820
Without that blanket, she can't take it easy.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.350
How awful!

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:23.470
Blanky!

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:27.530
I don't think it'll respond if you call out to it.

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:29.360
I'm sure it's sentient.

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:32.920
I see it! It's over there!

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:37.310
Wait!

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:43.840
We've distributed plenty of stimulus money, so why is it that the people don't spend any of it?

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:48.380
They must be worried about the future
and so deposit the cash into their savings.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:50.950
I think the people want a sense of security.

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:55.570
What's this?

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:56.870
I'm so sorry!

00:12:56.870 --> 00:12:58.790
I'm sorry for interrupting your political meeting!

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:01.790
I need that blanket! Komori can't feel secure without it!

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:03.790
Secure?

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:06.680
You can feel secure with this blanket?

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:07.160
Yes.

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:11.030
Teacher, I've got bad news!

00:13:11.030 --> 00:13:12.600
What did you say!?

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:17.140
The government is mass-producing Komori's
blanket and distributing them to the populace!

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:17.880
Government
announcement

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:20.540
A production line has already been built inside the school.

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:25.670
Komori's being wrapped for the sake of Japan!

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:28.150
Wrapped for the sake of Japan's economy!

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:33.350
It seems like people have begun spending stimulus money because they feel secure.

00:13:33.350 --> 00:13:36.530
Oh please, the economy won't recover with just that.

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:41.100
I feel secure! Let's spend our money!

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:43.370
No way!

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:47.290
Well, it's good to see the economy recovering.

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:49.220
It gets worse from here.

00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:50.240
You were there?

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:51.640
Yes, always.

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:55.600
Pressed by the recession, the rest of the world will aim for Komori!

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:57.730
Look, there's already a strange shadow!

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:00.500
We must protect her at all costs!

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:04.090
She's Japan's only hope for a successful economic recovery!

00:14:06.090 --> 00:14:09.410
But protecting her from professionals seems impossible!

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:11.160
I'm taking precautions.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:14.470
I'll gather everyone's comfort items to use as camouflage.

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:18.560
Wait, that's my pillow!

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:21.570
Please give it back! I can't sleep without that pillow!

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:23.240
She has our favorite items too!

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:25.040
There's Nami's diet supplements!

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:26.960
Kaere's English phrasebook.

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:28.860
Chiri's mysterious doll!

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.820
Kitsu, your approach is completely wrong!

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:35.090
Objects aren't what make people feel secure.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:39.420
What makes you really feel secure is knowing that
somebody more pathetic than yourself exists out there!

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:43.460
Girls feel relieved when they see
other girls uglier than themselves!

00:14:43.460 --> 00:14:44.090
Phew.

00:14:44.090 --> 00:14:46.970
Students feel relieved when they see other
students with worse grades than themselves!

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:47.630
Phew.

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:50.940
Manga authors feel relieved when they see
manga that are more boring than their own!

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:51.500
Phew.

00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:55.790
And don't you dare say that our publication is only
continuing for the sake of relieving other manga authors!

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:56.860
I wasn't going to!

00:14:57.360 --> 00:14:59.440
Such is the treachery of feeling relief!

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:04.740
Someone as despicable as me can never feel relief from looking at others.

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:06.650
There he goes again.

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:08.770
But Teacher, you of all people should feel secure,

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:09.650
since you're a civil servant.

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:12.010
Just because I'm a civil servant, it doesn't mean that...

00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:13.840
You don't have to worry about getting canned.

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:14.860
You should feel relieved.

00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:16.140
That's it.

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:19.130
If the citizens were all civil servants, they'd feel relieved and secure.

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:19.850
That's it!

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:23.220
That's never worked before, and considering it is even worse!

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:26.140
Besides, that's not even part of your party's platform!

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:29.860
We're impoverished now.

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:32.210
Let's go work in a neighboring country.

00:15:32.210 --> 00:15:37.130
When manga authors became civil servants, weekly publications became annual publications.

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:38.550
Those thieves.

00:15:38.550 --> 00:15:40.700
You can't blame them, since the price for paper skyrocketed.

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:44.080
With everyone dispossessed, reduced to the base of society,

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:46.620
what can we cling to for security?

00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:48.830
But we do feel secure.

00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:50.040
She's right!

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:50.890
Because...

00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:54.360
You make all of us feel secure.

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:56.470
So long as you're with us,

00:15:56.470 --> 00:15:57.840
we feel secure.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:15:59.370
I feel secure.

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:00.840
I feel completely relieved with you around.

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:02.410
Y-You make me feel safe.

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:07.200
G-Girls...

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:09.310
Thus, in order to establish a fair society,

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:11.520
let's distribute the wealth equally.

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:14.210
I want his eyes.

00:16:14.210 --> 00:16:16.060
I like his ears.

00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:18.180
I adore his tail.

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:19.980
I love his *****.

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:21.690
W-Wait!

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:24.060
Y'all think you're Abe Sada?

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:28.100
I'm relieved.

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:33.600
The grand prize for this year's slang competition has been announced.{both "slang" and "buzzword" are pretty bad words for this, but there's no fucking way I'm using "meme" and I can't think of anything better, so going with the one I already TSed}{maybe "catchphrase" but no one outside SA uses it that way}

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:37.840
Does the slang general wear a tracksuit and carry an umbrella on his back?

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:40.940
Silly Maria. It's a “prize”.

00:16:42.350 --> 00:16:45.570
If you say things like that, he'll actually appear.

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:51.990
Good!

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:56.590
The slang general really came!

00:16:56.590 --> 00:16:56.760
Stay true to the original

00:16:56.760 --> 00:16:56.930
Doesn't matter as long as it looks cool

00:16:56.930 --> 00:16:57.090
What a nice color

00:16:57.090 --> 00:16:57.260
What an interesting way to deal with it

00:16:57.260 --> 00:16:57.430
Sorry, but can you stay another hour

00:16:57.430 --> 00:16:57.690
Wait just five more minutes

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:02.970
The slang

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:03.970
general

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:05.570
really came!

00:17:05.570 --> 00:17:07.300
It's another gag where something shows up?

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:08.390
Well, I guess.

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:11.720
What are we supposed to do with a slang general!?

00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:12.780
If he exists,

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:14.660
then things like a record general,

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:16.130
or a comic general,

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:18.950
or a miraculous baseball tactical general must exist too.

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:20.360
Don't say it!

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:21.570
They'll come if you do!

00:17:25.590 --> 00:17:27.890
Move it, Gramps.

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:33.100
I'm sorry, but I can't allow outsiders to come on school property.

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:45.890
Where'd he go?

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:01.040
It seems they were stopped at the perimiter.

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:04.390
So they really came and even made it that far!?

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:09.100
Everyone says I'm popular, right?

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:10.070
Sure.

00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:11.840
Could I ask you something?

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:16.110
Is this year's slang prize popular among the generals?

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:22.070
It's “well, I guess”, what do you think?

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:22.900
I thought so.

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:24.210
So did I.

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:27.920
Maybe you should decide the slang prize yourself.

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:30.690
I-I should?

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:34.730
For example, these are the slang prize winners for the manga industry.

00:18:34.730 --> 00:18:35.890
No, don't!

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:37.940
I got it.

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:41.690
I'll think about creating my own slang.

00:18:41.690 --> 00:18:42.920
Impossible.

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:45.020
We must act now!

00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:47.970
Oh, they're the slang generals from '07!

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:50.770
This must be the '04 slang prize.

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:54.180
Feels good man. Feels reeeeaaaaaaal good.

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:56.250
I don't wanna to end up like them.

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:58.700
I'll never use slang again.

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:00.220
Good for you.

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:04.460
You'll become an object of abuse and
embarrassment just like us in no time.

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:06.790
It's over the second you're chosen.

00:19:06.790 --> 00:19:10.090
I won't become one of you.

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:14.200
I'm not like you!

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:18.200
Oh my.

00:19:19.670 --> 00:19:21.950
Such is the fate of a slang general.

00:19:21.950 --> 00:19:25.190
He couldn't escape the curse of slang.

00:19:25.410 --> 00:19:29.270
Here's my predictions for the 2010 Slang Prize!

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:11.480
Who's Kiyohiko?

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:14.010
Kiyohiko...?

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:16.790
It's Kiyohiko's Night tonight, right?

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:20.060
You mean Silent Night.

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:22.080
Kiyohiko's Night...

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:25.700
Kiyohiko's going to come?

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:32.640
I can't eat anymore...

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:39.980
Who're you!?

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:41.190
It's me, Kiyohiko.

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:43.570
Don't really show up!

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:46.020
Of course I came.

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:48.990
After all, it's Kiyohiko's Night.

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:52.670
Why are they jamming another story in so abruptly!?

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:55.830
So, how do you celebrate Kiyohiko's Night?

00:21:58.160 --> 00:21:59.760
How...?

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:02.670
It's nothing but a gag where you
show up, so it has to be like that!

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:04.670
Kiyohiko, believe in yourself!

00:22:04.670 --> 00:22:07.270
After all, it's Kiyohiko's Night.

00:22:08.210 --> 00:22:10.210
You're too kind, Kafuka.

00:22:10.210 --> 00:22:12.810
Do whatever your heart desires.

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:12.810
I...

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:14.950
Whatever my heart desires...

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:20.370
Now that I think of it, I can't think of anything I've ever wanted to do.

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:23.350
I've always lived strictly according to my parents' plan.

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:25.390
What are you talking about now!?

00:22:25.810 --> 00:22:28.650
Can I go get some Young Donuts?

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:29.790
Of course.

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:33.400
Can I name my firstborn Noah?

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:34.360
Of course.

00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:37.950
Can I take this party to LOFT/PLUS ONE?

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:40.430
You can do whatever you want!

00:22:40.430 --> 00:22:44.020
After all, tonight belongs to you!

00:22:47.410 --> 00:22:55.790
Kiyohiko's Night!

00:22:55.790 --> 00:23:04.190
Kiyohiko's Night!

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:26.530
I wish all the Kiyohikos out there a merry Christmas!

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:31.460
Just what kind of deer does a reindeer rein?

00:23:33.790 --> 00:23:35.130
Deer!?

00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:44.670
I think he tries a little harder in the weeklies.

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:51.680
Let's Kiyohiko night~

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:25.580
The Zetsubou-sensei Drawing Song is about to start.

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:27.930
Let's draw it calmly, okay?

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:35.790
A tulip leans in the wind, and lays flat.

00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:42.250
A tulip leans in the wind, and lays flat.

00:24:42.250 --> 00:24:43.820
Lightning...

00:24:43.820 --> 00:24:45.520
Flash!

00:24:45.520 --> 00:24:47.040
Lightning...

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:48.790
Flash!

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:51.880
The rain comes pouring down.

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:55.050
From the gaps came the leaks.

00:24:55.050 --> 00:24:58.310
Two puddles were made.

00:24:58.310 --> 00:25:01.480
Let's go fishing, there's the fish hook!

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:05.930
We made a fish cake with the fish we caught.

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:09.450
It's Zetsubou-sensei!

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:11.940
Did everyone draw him well?

00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:14.480
Bye bye!
